    (v_topic_id, 'A 70-old female presents with difficulty swallowing that initially only occurred with solid foods like steak and bread but has recently progressed to include liquids. They report significant weight loss. What is the most appropriate next step in the diagnostic algorithm?', '["Upper Endoscopy (EGD) with mucosal biopsies", "24-hour pH monitoring and esophageal impedance testing", "Empiric balloon dilation of the lower esophageal sphincter", "Barium swallow alone without further testing", "Esophageal manometry"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Esophageal dysphagia that initially affects only solids and then progresses to liquids suggests a mechanical obstruction (e.g., peptic stricture, esophageal ring, or esophageal carcinoma). The diagnostic algorithm for suspected mechanical obstruction requires an Upper Endoscopy (EGD) first to directly visualize the esophagus, obtain biopsies of any suspected mass or inflammation, and potentially perform therapeutic dilatation if a benign stricture is found.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 46-old lady with a history of alcohol use disorder presents with abdominal distention and a positive shifting dullness on percussion. A diagnostic paracentesis is performed. Lab analysis reveals ascites albumin of 1.2 g/dL and serum albumin of 2.6 g/dL. What is the calculated Serum-to-Ascites Albumin Gradient (SAAG), and what does it suggest?', '["SAAG is 0.46 g/dL, suggesting peritoneal carcinomatosis or tuberculosis", "SAAG is 2.1 g/dL, suggesting spontaneous bacterial peritonitis", "SAAG is 1.4 g/dL, suggesting portal hypertension (e.g., Cirrhosis, Heart Failure)", "SAAG is 1.4 g/dL, suggesting nephrotic syndrome or pancreatic ascites", "SAAG is 0.8 g/dL, suggesting portal hypertension"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The Serum-to-Ascites Albumin Gradient (SAAG) is calculated as: SAAG = Serum Albumin - Ascites Albumin. Here, SAAG = 2.6 g/dL - 1.2 g/dL = 1.4 g/dL. A SAAG >= 1.1 g/dL is highly suggestive of portal hypertension (cirrhosis, congestive heart failure, or Budd-Chiari syndrome). A SAAG < 1.1 g/dL suggests a non-portal hypertensive cause (peritoneal carcinomatosis, tuberculous peritonitis, nephrotic syndrome, or pancreatic ascites).'),
