    (v_topic_id, 'A 81-old female presents with difficulty swallowing that initially only occurred with solid foods like steak and bread but has recently progressed to include liquids. They report significant weight loss. What is the most appropriate next step in the diagnostic algorithm?', '["Esophageal manometry", "Empiric balloon dilation of the lower esophageal sphincter", "Barium swallow alone without further testing", "Upper Endoscopy (EGD) with mucosal biopsies", "24-hour pH monitoring and esophageal impedance testing"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Esophageal dysphagia that initially affects only solids and then progresses to liquids suggests a mechanical obstruction (e.g., peptic stricture, esophageal ring, or esophageal carcinoma). The diagnostic algorithm for suspected mechanical obstruction requires an Upper Endoscopy (EGD) first to directly visualize the esophagus, obtain biopsies of any suspected mass or inflammation, and potentially perform therapeutic dilatation if a benign stricture is found.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 84-old female with a history of alcohol use disorder presents with abdominal distention and a positive shifting dullness on percussion. A diagnostic paracentesis is performed. Lab analysis reveals ascites albumin of 1.2 g/dL and serum albumin of 2.6 g/dL. What is the calculated Serum-to-Ascites Albumin Gradient (SAAG), and what does it suggest?', '["SAAG is 0.46 g/dL, suggesting peritoneal carcinomatosis or tuberculosis", "SAAG is 2.1 g/dL, suggesting spontaneous bacterial peritonitis", "SAAG is 1.4 g/dL, suggesting nephrotic syndrome or pancreatic ascites", "SAAG is 1.4 g/dL, suggesting portal hypertension (e.g., Cirrhosis, Heart Failure)", "SAAG is 0.8 g/dL, suggesting portal hypertension"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The Serum-to-Ascites Albumin Gradient (SAAG) is calculated as: SAAG = Serum Albumin - Ascites Albumin. Here, SAAG = 2.6 g/dL - 1.2 g/dL = 1.4 g/dL. A SAAG >= 1.1 g/dL is highly suggestive of portal hypertension (cirrhosis, congestive heart failure, or Budd-Chiari syndrome). A SAAG < 1.1 g/dL suggests a non-portal hypertensive cause (peritoneal carcinomatosis, tuberculous peritonitis, nephrotic syndrome, or pancreatic ascites).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 28-old male undergoes a non-contrast abdominal CT scan for suspected nephrolithiasis, which reveals an incidental 2.5 cm, homogeneous, well-circumscribed right adrenal mass with low attenuation (<10 Hounsfield units). The patient is asymptomatic. What is the most appropriate next step in the workup algorithm?', '["Refer for immediate laparoscopic right adrenalectomy", "Reassure the patient and perform no further workup", "Perform biochemical screening to rule out a functional tumor (e.g., overnight dexamethasone suppression test, 24-hour urine metanephrines, aldosterone-to-renin ratio if hypertensive)", "Repeat the abdominal CT scan in 3 months to assess for growth", "Perform a CT-guided fine-needle aspiration biopsy of the adrenal mass"]'::jsonb, 2, 'All patients with an incidentally discovered adrenal mass (adrenal incidentaloma) must undergo biochemical evaluation to rule out a functional (hormone-secreting) tumor, regardless of whether they have symptoms. Screening includes: (1) an overnight 1 mg dexamethasone suppression test for subclinical Cushing''s, (2) 24-hour fractionated urinary metanephrines/catecholamines (or plasma free metanephrines) for pheochromocytoma, and (3) plasma aldosterone concentration to plasma renin activity ratio (if hypertensive) for Conn syndrome. Fine needle biopsy is contraindicated until pheochromocytoma is biochemically ruled out, due to risk of hypertensive crisis.');
